<!DOCTYPE html>
<!-- Quantum HTML + CSS example 20_components/20: Callout box -->
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>Callout box</title>
  <style>
    *, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }
    body {
      margin: 0;
      font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", Roboto, sans-serif;
      line-height: 1.5;
      color: #1f2933;
      background: #f5f7fa;
    }
    .demo { max-width: 60rem; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2rem 1.25rem; }
    .demo > h1 { margin: 0 0 0.25rem; font-size: 2rem; }
    .lead { margin: 0 0 2rem; color: #52606d; }

    .article { max-width: 40rem; }
    .callout { display: flex; gap: 1rem; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin: 1.5rem 0; border-radius: 14px; background: #f5f3ff; border: 1px solid #ddd6fe; }
    .callout strong { color: #6d28d9; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <main class="demo">
    <h1>Callout box</h1>
    <p class="lead">A highlighted note inside an article.</p>
    <article class="article">
      <p>Grid and flexbox solve different problems.</p>
      <aside class="callout"><span aria-hidden="true">&#128161;</span><p><strong>Tip:</strong> use grid for the page layout and flexbox for the pieces inside it.</p></aside>
      <p>You will often combine both in one page.</p>
    </article>
  </main>
</body>
</html>
